<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brazilian Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Empire of Brazil</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Brazilian Empire was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> from 1822 to 1889, founded when Pedro, son of the Portuguese king, declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>'s independence and became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-i-of-brazil">Emperor Pedro I</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-ii-of-brazil">Pedro II</a> it achieved relative stability through a constitution that gave the emperor a moderating power. It ended in 1889 when the military overthrew the monarchy, the year after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> was abolished.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire.jpg" alt="Brazilian Empire" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This vector image includes elements that have been taken or adapted from this file · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Brazil_(1853-1889).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Portugal claimed the territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> on 22 April 1500, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-alvares-cabral">Pedro Álvares Cabral</a> landed on its coast. In 1808 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s army invaded Portugal and forced the royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-braganza">House of Braganza</a> into exile. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-prince">The Prince</a> regent, later King John VI, established his government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a>, which became the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a>. When John VI returned to Portugal he left his eldest son Pedro to rule the Kingdom of Brazil as regent. On 7 September 1822 Pedro declared Brazil independent and, after a war against his father's kingdom, was acclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-i-of-brazil">Emperor Pedro I</a> on 12 October.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-i-of-brazil">Pedro I</a> clashed with a sizeable parliamentary faction over the role of the monarch. The unsuccessful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cisplatine-war">Cisplatine War</a> ended in 1828 with the secession of Cisplatina, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a>. He also became king of Portugal in 1826, and, unable to handle both countries, abdicated the Brazilian throne on 7 April 1831 and left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. His five-year-old son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-ii-of-brazil">Pedro II</a> succeeded under a weak regency, and the lack of a ruling monarch to arbitrate led to regional civil wars. By 1846 Pedro II had matured and taken control, and he called the conservatives to form a government in 1848.</p><p>From the 1850s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> enjoyed stability and prosperity, with railways, the electric <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> and steamship lines. The Brazilian victories in the Platine War, the Uruguayan War and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguayan-war">Paraguayan War</a>, which ended in 1870, marked a golden age. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> was restricted by successive laws and abolished by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lei-aurea">Golden Law</a>, signed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabel-princess-imperial-of-brazil">Princess Isabel</a> on 13 May 1888. Its end ended the belief of the ultraconservatives, backed by powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> farmers, in the crown's neutrality. They shifted to republicanism. Historians attribute the main responsibility for the fall of the monarchy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-ii-of-brazil">Pedro II</a>, who grew indifferent to its fate; after a 58-year reign he was overthrown in a military coup on 15 November 1889.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Constitution of 1824 created legislative, moderating, executive and judicial branches. The emperor headed the moderating and executive branches but had few ways of imposing his will on the General Assembly, apart from dissolving it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> was the state religion, and the emperor controlled church affairs. Slaves made up 29 per cent of the population in 1823 and fewer than 5 per cent by 1887. By 1889 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> had 9,200 km of railway track. Noble titles were not hereditary, except for the imperial family.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Empire of Brazil” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A congress at Florida declared the Banda Oriental independent of the <b>Empire of Brazil</b> and united with the River Plate provinces.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-25">25 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After British mediation, the <b>Empire of Brazil</b> and the United Provinces of the Río de la Plata signed the Preliminary Peace Convention.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-27">27 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>During the unstable regency period in the <b>Empire of Brazil</b>, discontent in Salvador over central control and military recruitment for the war in Rio Grande do Sul came to a head.</blockquote><a href="/state/bahia-republic">Bahia Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>Bahia became a province of the <b>Empire of Brazil</b>, though Portuguese troops held Salvador until 2 July 1823, still celebrated as Bahia's independence day.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-of-bahia">Captaincy of Bahia</a></li><li><blockquote>Pernambuco led the anti-Portuguese Republic of Pernambuco in 1817, and became a province of the <b>Empire of Brazil</b> after independence in 1822.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-of-pernambuco">Captaincy of Pernambuco</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1821 the captaincy became a province, and in 1822 Rio was the capital of the independent <b>Empire of Brazil</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-of-rio-de-janeiro">Captaincy of Rio de Janeiro</a></li></ul></section>
